From 98b5639d10a28c931edd0365d45fa58307a5ae7d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shaun Adams <s.adams@f5.com>
Date: Tue, 16 Jun 2026 19:08:19 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] mt-runtime-isolation: per-tenant Bifrost runtime dispatch +
 eviction hooks
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closes Stage 1's runtime-isolation gap. Patches 1-6 gave us data-scope
isolation via the GORM tenant callback, but inference still dispatched
through a single shared root *bifrost.Bifrost — provider workers,
queues, and the lib.Config.Providers in-memory snapshot were
last-write-wins across tenants. This commit wires multitenant.Manager
(introduced in patch1) into the inference path so each tenant gets its
own engine, snapshotting providers+keys from a tenant-scoped DB read at
Acquire time.

Sync inference:
  - TenantDispatcherMiddleware mounts after TenantResolverMiddleware in
    RegisterInferenceRoutes; reads the tenant id off ctx, asks the
    dispatcher (BifrostHTTPServer.BifrostFor) for the right runtime,
    stashes it on ctx UserValue, defers release. Streaming-safe — defer
    fires after the SSE drains.
  - inference.go and mcpinference.go gain h.bf(ctx) helpers that read
    the per-request runtime off ctx, falling back to the constructor-
    time h.client. All 48 h.client.X(bifrostCtx, ...) call sites in
    those two files refactored.

Async (request lifetime ≠ closure lifetime):
  - AsyncHandler.SetDispatcher wired at boot. Each submit method calls
    acquireBifrost(ctx) BEFORE SubmitJob — holds an extra refcount that
    survives past request return. The closure defers release(); the
    SubmitJob err path explicitly releases since the closure never ran.

Eviction:
  - server.EvictTenantRuntime exposed as the package-level
    handlers.TenantEvict hook (wired in initializeManager). All 6
    provider/key admin write handlers call defer EvictTenant(ctx) so
    the next Acquire re-snapshots fresh DB state. Over-eager evict on
    error paths is acceptable — same cost as a single startup load.

Single-tenant fallback: BifrostFor returns s.Client+noop for empty /
"default" tids or when Manager is nil; OSS deployments see zero overhead.

Docs: docs/multi-tenant-f5/02-stage2-runtime-isolation.md.

Known gaps (Stage 3): per-tenant MCP isolation, WS/WebRTC/Realtime
handlers still on root runtime, Manager LRU cap unset, Manager unit
tests.
